<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shimazu Yoshihiro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1535">1535</a>–<a href="/years/1619">1619</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shimazu Yoshihiro was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihisa">Shimazu Yoshihisa</a>. He fought in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, won a famous victory at Sacheon during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1598, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600 led his small force on a desperate breakout through the victorious Tokugawa army. Satsuma kept its lands through negotiation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihiro.jpg" alt="Shimazu Yoshihiro" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 不明。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shimazu_Yoshihiro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yoshihiro was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-takahisa">Shimazu Takahisa</a> and is said to have been born in Izaku Castle. As lord of Iino Castle he defeated the Itō clan with Niiro Tadamoto at the Battle of Kizaki in 1572, and with his brothers beat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-clan">Otomo clan</a> at the Battle of Mimigawa in 1578. In 1587, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>'s armies came to subdue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>, he pressed for war even after his brother Yoshihisa had surrendered, and gave in only after repeated requests. He was long believed to have become the 17th head of the clan, but it is now thought that Yoshihisa kept that position.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Hideyoshi he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in both 1592 and 1597. In 1597, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todo-takatora">Tōdō Takatora</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-yoshiaki">Katō Yoshiaki</a> and Konishi Yukinaga, he defeated the navy of Wŏn Kyun, and at Sacheon in 1598 his 7,000 soldiers beat a Ming army of 37,000. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-noryang">Battle of Noryang</a>, the last of the war, he sought to cross the strait and join Konishi; afterwards he rescued the Japanese commanders and returned home.</p><p>Recent research holds that he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> from the start, not after a slight at Fushimi as novels of the Edo Period claimed. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> his 1,500 men held their ground without fighting. Stranded among at least 30,000 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>'s troops, he was dissuaded by his nephew Shimazu Toyohisa from a last charge and instead cut through the enemy in a fighting retreat known as sutegamari, in which Toyohisa and most of his men died and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a> was wounded. He then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> by ship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1602 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> allowed the clan to keep its domain and let Yoshihiro's son Shimazu Tadatsune succeed him. Yoshihiro retired to Sakurajima and taught the younger generation. A devoted Buddhist, he had raised a monument to enemy dead of the Korean war. When he died in 1619, several retainers who had fought beside him took their own lives, and in 1918 Emperor Taishō gave him the posthumous senior third rank.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shimazu Yoshihiro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Takahisa's son Yoshihisa and his brothers <b>Yoshihiro</b>, Toshihisa and Iehisa formed a military team.</blockquote><a href="/state/shimazu-clan-domain">Shimazu clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.6,130.56&amp;year=1589" aria-label="Kagoshima on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7917 1013 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shimazu Yoshihiro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8367 1283h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8367 1283h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.6,130.56&amp;year=1589">What was here in 1589? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
